description:
I want to use `in_parallel()` in my package code to enable users to download data in parallel. To do this, users would need `{mirai}` and `{carrier}` installed.

It seems like the best practice would be to make these packages optional dependencies (i.e., list them in `Suggests`, not `Imports`). Then, the package code would use `in_parallel()` only if a user has the "parallel packages" installed.

Exporting `parallel_pkgs_installed()` would enable package developers to use that function to check for the packages, instead of re-writing these checks ourselves. It would also protect our code against future changes to the dependencies of `in_parallel()`.

Here is a simplified version of my usage:

```r
optional_parallel_map <- function(fun, nums) {
  .f <- if (
    # current:
    rlang::is_installed(c("carrier", "mirai"), version = c("0.3.0",  "2.5.1"))
    # desired:
    # purrr::parallel_pkgs_installed()
  ) {
    purrr::in_parallel(function(.num) fun(.num), fun = fun)
  } else {
    function(.num) fun(.num)
  }

  purrr::map(nums, .f = .f)
}
```

**If there is a better way for me to implement "optional parallelism," please let me know!**

I run a lot of simulations, and relied heavily on rerun().  The current view is this should be replaced with map( 1:R, ~ one_run() ) rather than rerun( R, one_run() ).  But this makes it hard to pass arguments through to one_run() since the map version makes the anonymous function take an extra ID number, which is often not particularly useful for the one_run() call.

E.g., consider passing through triple dots like this:

```
func = function( x, ... ) {
  cat( "func - x = ", x, "\n" )
  print( list( ... ) )
  
}

func_wrap <- function( r, ... ) {

  cat( "func_wrap:\n" )
  print( list( ... ) )
  
  func( 10, ... )
  
  res <- map( r:(r+1), ~ {
    cat( "** map tick\n" )
    func( x=4, ... ) } )
  
  invisible( res )
}


func_wrap( 4, a=2, b=3 )
```

This has ties to the behavior noted in https://github.com/tidyverse/purrr/issues/1118 (which I flagged before, but this keeps coming up for me--I am posting this as it seems a different take on the original issue)

I discovered that `fs::path_file` and `fs::path_dir` run very slowly on windows (see `fs` issue 424), and since most of my use case of these functions is after using `readr::read_csv(files, .id="file_path")`, most of the vector is duplication. As such, I found that I could save a significant amount of time by deduplicating the vector (2x on Mac, 40x on Windows). This approach is not just helpful for `fs::path_` functions.

The most straightforward approach is:

```r
with_deduplication <- function(f) {
  function(x, ...) {
    ux <- unique(x)
    f(ux, ...)[match(x, ux)]
  }
} 
```

I've also submitted a PR into `vctrs` to speed this up (see `vctrs` issue 1857 and PR 1858).

