The `rbernoulli` function will work, and produce incorrect answers when `n` is a scalar and `prob` is a vector with length > 1. 
```{rconsole}
> purrr::rbernoulli(1, p = c(0.1, 0.2, 0.3))
[1] FALSE FALSE FALSE
```
Since `rbernoulli` is implemented as
```{r}
stats::runif(n) > (1 - p)
```
the code above will only make one draw from the uniform distribution, but then recycle `n` to compare with `p` and return a vector the same size as `p`.  This is a pretty subtle bug and can easily burn users who may assume that `rbernoulli(1, p)` will draw one sample each for each value of `p`.

Base R seems to have the following semantics for distribution sampling functions:

- if `n` has length 1, treat as the number of samples
- if `n` has length > 1, use the length of `n` as the number of samples
- if `n == 0`, return a zero-length vector.

In `rbinom`, when `length(prob) > n`, it simply uses the first `n` values of `prob`.
```{r}
> rbinom(1, size = 1, prob = c(0.1, 0.2, 0.3))
[1] 0
```

The following checks in `rbernoulli` should address these issues:
```{r}
rbernoulli <- function(n, p = 0.5) {
  # cannot let runif catch problems with n because need the value of n in order
  # to truncate prob
  if (length(n) == 0) {
    return(logical(0))
  } else if (length(n) == 1 && n == 0) {
    return(logical(0))
  } else if (length(n) > 1) {
    n <- length(n)
  }
  # truncate to at most n probs
  if (length(p) > n) {
    p <- p[seq_len(n)]
  }
  stats::runif(n) > (1 - p)
}
```
```{rconsole}
> rbernoulli(0)
logical(0)
> rbernoulli(NULL)
logical(0)
> rbernoulli(1)
[1] FALSE
> rbernoulli(5)
[1]  TRUE FALSE FALSE  TRUE  TRUE
> rbernoulli(2, c(0.1, 0.2))
[1] FALSE FALSE
> rbernoulli(c("a", "b"), c(0.1, 0.2))
[1] FALSE FALSE
> rbernoulli(1, c(0.1, 0.2))
[1] FALSE
```

